e nature of society and how that  relates to conflict and my argument is  that that there are basically two kinds  of wars there are Wars about rulership  and that there are wars about the rules  and I try and use this to explain why it  is that you get variation in the  intensity of violence why it is that  some wars are more violent more  destructive and less easy to solve than  others and I basically argue and i apply  insights from the political economy of  institutions to the study of conflict so  so in effect my work is an introduction  of clouds of its to the political  economy of institutions okay and in your  paper there are three sections where you  structure your argument tell us about  that yeah i mean the the unfortunately  clouds of it's even though his his most  famous aphorism you know war is a it's  the continuation of politics by other  means isn't well known his work is  actually not read by by contemporary  american political scientists and so the  first section is really to try and  remind people what it is that he said in  why he said it and it's important to  revisit not just the historical context  but the theoretical conclusions that he  drew from from it and so the first  section in a sense deals with the french  revolution and its impact on on europe  and how it is that Clausewitz and his  milieu because it wasn't simply clouds  of it but various people within the  prussian high command within within  within the austrian within the austrian  establishment who really had to do some  deep thinking about why it is that they  were losing why it is that they were  being catastrophic Lee defeated and what  it is that they would need to do to  respond to it so that's the first  section the second section then moves  beyond clouds of its  and and the first section is not saying  anything original about clouds of its I  mean it's it's a fairly straightforward  rendition of his work and his times the  second section is where it really wears  you know is the real contribution that  I'm making and that is to lay out in  institutional terms what it is that's  going on now Klaus if it's had the  insight that there is a difference  between limited war and total war but he  died before he could fully develop that  and nobody has really taken an up you  know where he left off and so the second  section and you know again this is what  in a sense what what I'm really  contributing to to to the study of war  is to try and lay this out in rigorous  social science terms but what this means  i mean why it is that you would get  limited and why it is that you would get  total war and that's the dimension and  that's the well that's the section in  which i developed this institutional  theory of war in effect arguing that we  cannot understand war we cannot  understand conflict without  understanding the underlying social  institutions that are being contested or  not in the case of limited war so the  basic argument there is that there that  you would get two kinds of conflicts the  first type of conflict is about rank and  status and the argument is that  conflicts about rank and status are  analogues to phenomenon that we would  find in primatology in any kind of  social hierarchy and that is that there  will always be conflict about who is at  the top of the heap and my argument is  that conflicts about that about whether  who's going to be the Pope or who is  going to be the president or who's going  to be the leader nine that actually will  be limited right it will be limited  because very little else is going to  change right it wouldn't matter in a  coup in a dictatorship whether we know  one general or the other rules or rather  that affects a very very small number of  people and therefore the violence it  tends to generate is limited on the  other side and the  the more interesting I or at least what  I think is the more interesting form of  conflict is the total war what  Clausewitz would have called total war  