l science literature is on conflict  have fundamentally missed some basic  basic conceptual distinctions that need  to be made in order to explain the sorts  of things that we would like to explain  in effect I'm saying that what we  require is a much deeper understanding  of the sociology of conflict in order to  have the kinds of theories that we would  like to have so that would be the third  section okay and what is your  implication for your social theory of  war well the hubris of the project is  that is that I can explain a great deal  more than than than others but I'll give  you a few a few examples the first  example would be that there's a tendency  both in the public domain but also in  the social science literature is on  conflict to either talk about about  conflict from the perspective of states  or from the perspective of individual  leaders that are making decisions and  there is been there has been very little  attempt to unify these different levels  of analysis and the basic argument that  I'm making is that it is in fact  possible to deal with at least two  levels of analysis at the individual  level as well as the societal level in a  unified theory and so the first  implication is that there's much greater  explanatory power but if you want to  think about this in terms of current  debates from the perspective of people  who are concerned about religious  conflict there's a tendency to to to  deal with religious conflict from the  perspective of what motivates individual  actors and there's utility in that right  so so the way the question tends to get  framed in in the public domain is well  what is it about these five lines in the  Quran or in the Bible that would lead  somebody to go blow themselves up and on  on the streets and what I'm saying is  that well that may not necessarily be  the most truthful way to go about  looking at the problem that what what is  much more important for understanding  patterns of violence is what it is that 