 sort of internal debates within  political science and so the general  trends over the last 10 to 15 years has  been to move to the micro level and that  is to look at individual level factors  in explaining conflict and I'm saying  that well I mean while there is a great  deal of utility in that what we need to  do is to spend much more time thinking  about groups because war is not simply  about two individuals in conflict war is  about groups and we cannot we cannot  hope to understand war as distinctive  from from fighting between two  individuals unless we take the sociology  of group behavior seriously and I and  and I that's the agenda that I'm pushing  within within the social sciences and in  terms of in terms of a common framework  again this is a this relates to to how  it is that the social sciences have been  have been dealing with conflict there  are a number of distinct research  communities within within the social  sciences within anthropology with in  history within political science and to  a lesser extent with in sociology and  these are all distinctive communities  distinctive research communities that  are not talking to one another and I  think that we are in effect constantly  reinventing the wheel and each of these  sub in T sub research communities  without actually making progress and of  course as a political scientist I really  do believe that we in political science  have a great deal to learn from  historians from anthropologist and from  sociologists and so I'm trying to  demonstrate to to my colleagues what why  it is that they  read history why it is that they should  read anthropology why they should be  aware of Faber and marks and Durkheim  from from sociology so if everyone in  those different disciplines worked to  get together we might come up with more  solutions to the problems we face I  certainly think we'll do a better job  than we're doing now yeah okay well  thank you very much for being with us  here today and sharing some of your  research well thank you for having me  for more information about professor  Sharma and his work please visit our  website at yale edu backslash Macmillan  report for sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you

